1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:04,000
What are the world's greatest inventions?

2
00:00:04,001 --> 00:00:07,000
How far back can we trace their origins?

3
00:00:07,001 --> 00:00:10,000
Which inventions have had the biggest impact on our world?

4
00:00:10,001 --> 00:00:14,000
And what does the future hold for these amazing devices?

5
00:00:14,001 --> 00:00:19,000
Now, Great Inventions on Modern Marvels.

6
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:39,500
In today's world, technology and invention progress at an astonishing speed.

7
00:00:39,501 --> 00:00:45,960
We have instantaneous access to information and images from around the globe.

8
00:00:45,961 --> 00:00:49,960
Advances in modern medicine are working to vanquish even the most elusive and deadly

9
00:00:49,961 --> 00:00:50,960
of diseases.

10
00:00:50,961 --> 00:00:59,600
We can travel great distances in a matter of hours and travel beyond Earth into space.

11
00:00:59,601 --> 00:01:05,600
All of this is possible because of humankind's desire and ability to invent.

12
00:01:05,601 --> 00:01:11,600
But many of our greatest modern inventions often had humble ancient origins,

13
00:01:11,601 --> 00:01:16,600
key discoveries that led the way for great inventions to follow.

14
00:01:16,601 --> 00:01:19,600
But what makes a great invention?

15
00:01:19,601 --> 00:01:24,600
Great invention has a kind of elegance and a kind of projection.

16
00:01:24,601 --> 00:01:26,600
It doesn't solve one problem.

17
00:01:26,601 --> 00:01:27,552
one problem.

18
00:01:27,553 --> 00:01:33,340
It opens up the opportunity to be applied to a variety of problems and it

19
00:01:33,341 --> 00:01:35,371
opens up new opportunities.

20
00:01:35,372 --> 00:01:39,960
One invention that has played a key role in many of society's

21
00:01:39,961 --> 00:01:46,592
greatest inventions is also one of history's oldest technological devices, the wheel.

22
00:01:46,593 --> 00:01:47,293
Though

23
00:01:47,294 --> 00:01:52,060
the exact origins of the wheel as we know it are unknown, it is believed that the combined

24
00:01:52,061 --> 00:01:58,560
use of wheel and axle as a device for transport was first seen around 3000 BC in the ancient

25
00:01:58,561 --> 00:02:00,380
civilization of Mesopotamia.

26
00:02:00,381 --> 00:02:04,020
What's really important is the wheel as used on farmer's

27
00:02:04,021 --> 00:02:09,580
cars allows for the movement of crops from the farmlands into the cities and the more efficient

28
00:02:09,581 --> 00:02:15,280
that you can move crops and foodstuffs in from the farmlands into the cities the bigger the

29
00:02:15,281 --> 00:02:16,818
population you can have.

30
00:02:16,819 --> 00:02:21,240
But the wheel had even more ancient uses with the discovery of rotary

31
00:02:21,241 --> 00:02:28,540
motion as early as 8000 BC, enterprising inventors of times long past found the wheel to be an

32
00:02:28,541 --> 00:02:30,383
important and versatile tool.

33
00:02:30,384 --> 00:02:34,640
They began using forms of the wheel to aid in their everyday tasks.

34
00:02:36,040 --> 00:02:42,340
Flywheels and pulleys allowed for heavy stones and rock to be lifted, allowing cities to rise up

35
00:02:42,341 --> 00:02:45,081
quicker, higher, and more efficiently.

36
00:02:45,082 --> 00:02:49,120
Waterwheels became a primary source for generating early

37
00:02:49,121 --> 00:02:55,540
forms of power. And rotating gears became the essential device in an endless variety

38
00:02:55,541 --> 00:03:00,761
of tools and machinery, helping to spark the Industrial Revolution.

39
00:03:00,762 --> 00:03:02,320
The wheel and rotary

40
00:03:02,321 --> 00:03:10,920
motion made the creation of another civilization changing invention possible, the steam engine.

41
00:03:10,921 --> 00:03:16,320
The steam engine propelled the world into a new age of transportation.

42
00:03:16,321 --> 00:03:17,400
Popular legend

43
00:03:17,401 --> 00:03:22,480
It has it that 18th century inventor James Watt discovered the power of steam when he

44
00:03:22,481 --> 00:03:26,060
observed the lifting lid of a kettle of boiling water.

45
00:03:26,061 --> 00:03:31,180
The truth is that man had been aware of the power of steam for countless centuries, long

46
00:03:31,181 --> 00:03:35,360
before Watt ever sat down for his first cup of tea.

47
00:03:35,361 --> 00:03:41,000
As early as 100 A.D., ancient writings told of a rotating steam turbine wheel created

48
00:03:41,001 --> 00:03:43,840
by a hero of Alexandria.

49
00:03:43,841 --> 00:03:47,580
The device was described simply as a sphere of Aeolus,

50
00:03:47,581 --> 00:03:51,080
but Hero never realized the potential uses of his creation

51
00:03:51,081 --> 00:03:53,620
and dismissed it as nothing more than a simple toy

52
00:03:53,621 --> 00:03:56,080
to keep a drowsy emperor awake.

53
00:03:57,520 --> 00:03:59,560
It would be nearly 2,000 years

54
00:03:59,561 --> 00:04:02,720
before a practical steam engine would be invented.

55
00:04:03,620 --> 00:04:05,460
The steam engine was originally conceived

56
00:04:05,461 --> 00:04:08,860
to pump water out of very deep mines,

57
00:04:08,861 --> 00:04:13,720
and it soon became applied to lots of other things,

58
00:04:14,140 --> 00:04:16,980
especially for driving all sorts of mills.

59
00:04:17,840 --> 00:04:21,660
Most closely associated with the creation of the steam engine

60
00:04:21,661 --> 00:04:25,800
is 18th century English engineer and inventor Thomas Newcomen.

61
00:04:26,400 --> 00:04:32,600
Newcomen designed and built one of the first practical atmospheric steam engines around 1712.

62
00:04:33,260 --> 00:04:37,620
The steam engine consisted of a series of steam-driven piston rods

63
00:04:37,621 --> 00:04:41,700
linked by chains that rose and fell in order to lift water out of mines.

64
00:04:42,760 --> 00:04:46,320
For over 50 years, Newcomen's steam engine set the standard,

65
00:04:46,900 --> 00:04:49,940
but a Scottish instrument maker and engineer, James Watt,

66
00:04:50,040 --> 00:04:52,280
set out to improve on Newcomen's creation.

67
00:04:52,900 --> 00:04:57,320
Initially, the rotative motion wasn't used with atmospheric engines

68
00:04:57,321 --> 00:04:59,360
because they weren't very evenly running engines.

69
00:04:59,361 --> 00:05:02,600
James Watt, by applying steam to both sides of the piston in a cylinder

70
00:05:02,601 --> 00:05:05,420
and with the use of properly designed valves,

71
00:05:05,421 --> 00:05:11,020
could impart a regular rocking motion to a beam and by means of a crank, then rotate a wheel.

72
00:05:11,680 --> 00:05:18,020
This improved steam engine, aided by the wheel, provided a major advance in the way we traveled over water.

73
00:05:18,740 --> 00:05:23,480
By the late 1700s, the steamship was man's first major development

74
00:05:23,481 --> 00:05:28,640
at traveling great distances since the creation of the sail thousands of years before.

75
00:05:29,900 --> 00:05:34,820
The French were the steamship's earliest pioneers in the late 1700s,

76
00:05:34,821 --> 00:05:45,001
But it was an American, Robert Fulton, who first achieved commercial success when he gained worldwide attention for driving his steamship the Claremont between New

77
00:05:45,002 --> 00:05:46,500
York and Albany in 1807.

78
00:05:46,960 --> 00:05:54,060
With the success of steam travel on the waterways, inventors looked at the steam engine as a source of power for land transport.

79
00:05:55,180 --> 00:06:02,860
The steam engine led to another revolutionary invention that literally connected communities the world over, the railroad.

80
00:06:02,861 --> 00:06:11,860
The powerful steam locomotives created in the early 1800s represented an unprecedented leap over early forms of railroads.

81
00:06:12,860 --> 00:06:16,860
The first railroads would not have been steam powered, they would have been animal powered.

82
00:06:16,861 --> 00:06:19,860
Animals were used to pull strings of cars on rails.

83
00:06:19,861 --> 00:06:26,860
But those early developments of steam power made it more efficient to pull heavier loads further distances.

84
00:06:26,861 --> 00:06:39,024
In the 1860s, America was literally tied together with the building of the Transcontinental Railroad from Nebraska to the golden, yet isolated, land of promise,

85
00:06:39,025 --> 00:06:39,860
California.

86
00:06:39,861 --> 00:06:50,860
The use of the steam engine helped steel-wheeled railroads become an increasingly powerful force in the growth and development of cities around the world.

87
00:06:50,861 --> 00:06:56,860
Railways soon became the world's dominant mode of freight, shipping, and transportation.

88
00:06:56,861 --> 00:07:03,860
One of the things that the railroad did was give people mobility that they'd never had before,

89
00:07:03,861 --> 00:07:06,860
allowed them to travel long distances.

90
00:07:06,861 --> 00:07:11,860
By the late 1800s, steam power ruled transport,

91
00:07:11,861 --> 00:07:17,860
but inventors began looking for alternative, more efficient means of transportation.

92
00:07:17,861 --> 00:07:23,520
The result was one of the most pervasive and important inventions in history,

93
00:07:24,140 --> 00:07:28,880
an invention that would soon dominate the transportation industry, the automobile.

94
00:07:30,780 --> 00:07:36,000
The early automobiles originally used the steam engine as their main source of power,

95
00:07:36,380 --> 00:07:43,080
but they were slow, cumbersome, and fairly uncomfortable for the passengers forced to sit close to the steamy hot boiler.

96
00:07:43,081 --> 00:07:51,840
the very first automobile is generally credited to 18th century french inventor nicholas joseph

97
00:07:51,841 --> 00:07:57,480
cugnot his vehicle was financed by the french government and was originally designed as a steam

98
00:07:57,481 --> 00:08:04,260
propelled three-wheeled artillery wagon with a top speed of two miles per hour the vehicle ran out of

99
00:08:04,261 --> 00:08:11,880
control during a demonstration and crashed into a wall but it is 1880s german inventor and automaker

100
00:08:11,881 --> 00:08:15,920
Carl Benz, who is generally credited as the father of the automobile.

101
00:08:17,000 --> 00:08:23,040
Carl Benz and his contemporary Gottlieb Daimler are important because they're really the first

102
00:08:23,041 --> 00:08:28,940
people to not only come up with, in their case, gasoline-powered road vehicles, but

103
00:08:28,941 --> 00:08:34,280
they persisted at it and eventually developed vehicles that they sold for market.

104
00:08:34,940 --> 00:08:40,560
One man, who perhaps more than any other would revolutionize the automobile industry

105
00:08:40,561 --> 00:08:45,260
with his gasoline-powered automobiles was Detroit automaker Henry Ford.

106
00:08:45,860 --> 00:08:50,580
Ironically, it was an early steam engine that inspired the automotive pioneer.

107
00:08:52,160 --> 00:08:57,040
The first self-powered vehicle that Henry Ford ever saw as a boy

108
00:08:57,041 --> 00:08:58,700
was one of these steam traction engines.

109
00:08:59,360 --> 00:09:02,840
For Ford, one of these lightbulb moments,

110
00:09:02,841 --> 00:09:05,760
he saw this thing coming down the road as a young boy,

111
00:09:06,040 --> 00:09:08,420
and it affected him profoundly.

112
00:09:08,421 --> 00:09:13,860
By the early 1900s, numerous companies were beginning to make automobiles,

113
00:09:14,200 --> 00:09:18,240
but it was Henry Ford who succeeded in bringing the auto to the public.

114
00:09:20,320 --> 00:09:25,760
Ford's great contribution was to really see where the future of the market lay,

115
00:09:26,020 --> 00:09:29,580
which turned out to be in cheap cars for the masses.

116
00:09:30,040 --> 00:09:35,420
And he developed not only a cheap car, but a very good car, his Model T Ford,

117
00:09:35,421 --> 00:09:40,520
and developed the assembly line process for producing it cheaper.

118
00:09:41,560 --> 00:09:46,960
Ford's assembly line concept had been inspired by a somewhat more gruesome counterpart.

119
00:09:48,560 --> 00:09:52,800
The meatpacking industry was often called a disassembly line,

120
00:09:52,801 --> 00:09:55,740
where the carcass of an animal moved by on a conveyor

121
00:09:55,741 --> 00:09:58,680
and the various meat cutters took pieces out of the animal.

122
00:09:59,120 --> 00:10:01,560
The great accomplishment of Ford Motor Company

123
00:10:01,561 --> 00:10:05,480
was to take something that was enormously complicated like an automobile

124
00:10:05,481 --> 00:10:08,440
and put it on this assembly line method,

125
00:10:08,740 --> 00:10:11,420
which required interchangeable parts,

126
00:10:11,580 --> 00:10:15,580
required all these conveyors and carefully choreographed movement

127
00:10:15,581 --> 00:10:18,300
of all these parts and sub-assemblies.

128
00:10:19,200 --> 00:10:22,920
Engines and transmissions and rear axles had to be on their own assembly lines

129
00:10:22,921 --> 00:10:26,160
before they could all come together for that chassis assembly line.

130
00:10:26,161 --> 00:10:28,180
But it did come together,

131
00:10:28,820 --> 00:10:32,200
With the assembly line in full force by the 1920s,

132
00:10:32,460 --> 00:10:35,580
the price of the Model T, or Tin Lizzy as it was known,

133
00:10:35,581 --> 00:10:41,700
had dropped from $850, a staggering figure for the day, to only $260.

134
00:10:42,880 --> 00:10:46,680
By the time the Model T stopped production in 1927,

135
00:10:47,200 --> 00:10:50,040
17 million had rolled off the line.

136
00:10:50,200 --> 00:10:54,360
The automobile is probably the most influential invention

137
00:10:54,361 --> 00:10:57,400
of the first half of the 20th century.

138
00:10:57,401 --> 00:11:00,240
It had such huge ramifications.

139
00:11:00,860 --> 00:11:02,320
It reshapes the landscape,

140
00:11:02,580 --> 00:11:04,900
end up with things like interstate highway system.

141
00:11:05,260 --> 00:11:09,480
It results in, not to death, but the decline of the railroads,

142
00:11:09,560 --> 00:11:11,900
which were the great 19th century industry.

143
00:11:12,660 --> 00:11:15,380
The enormous popularity of the automobile

144
00:11:15,381 --> 00:11:17,800
would forever alter transportation.

145
00:11:18,400 --> 00:11:20,520
And perhaps more than any other device,

146
00:11:20,780 --> 00:11:22,880
it is the wheel that helped facilitate

147
00:11:22,881 --> 00:11:26,140
the transportation and industrial revolution.

148
00:11:27,400 --> 00:11:31,478
There are nearly 300 million cars in the U.S.

149
00:11:31,479 --> 00:11:36,280
alone, over 40% of the world's automobile population.

150
00:11:37,080 --> 00:11:40,400
Great Inventions will return on Modern Marvels.

151
00:11:43,920 --> 00:11:49,480
Many key inventions, such as the wheel, were a product of discovery, often accidental,

152
00:11:50,060 --> 00:11:54,080
by practical beings looking for solutions to everyday problems.

153
00:11:54,081 --> 00:12:01,080
By contrast, the study and harnessing of electricity was a direct result of scientific research.

154
00:12:01,081 --> 00:12:08,080
Scientists in ancient Greece learned that if you rubbed amber or electron in the Greek language,

155
00:12:08,081 --> 00:12:12,080
it created a magnetic force that would attract light objects such as feathers.

156
00:12:12,081 --> 00:12:19,080
Viewed as a mere amusement, these ancient Greeks had no idea how to harness this force.

157
00:12:19,081 --> 00:12:27,320
It was not until roughly 200 years ago that scientists finally began to discover viable methods of harnessing electricity.

158
00:12:28,680 --> 00:12:36,600
Perhaps most famous was Benjamin Franklin, who in 1752 flew a kite in the midst of an electrical storm.

159
00:12:37,460 --> 00:12:42,700
He first noticed that strands of thread stood on end, as if suspended on a common conductor.

160
00:12:42,701 --> 00:12:47,980
When Franklin touched the attached metal key, he received quite an electrical shock.

161
00:12:47,981 --> 00:12:54,140
With this jolt, Franklin had proven that electricity was manifested in the clouds in the form of lightning.

162
00:12:55,200 --> 00:12:58,720
Franklin wrote in his own publication, Poor Richard's Almanac,

163
00:12:59,080 --> 00:13:03,460
that his invention of the lightning rod could secure houses from lightning blasts

164
00:13:03,461 --> 00:13:09,880
by directing the powerful bolt of electricity into the grounded metal rod instead of the structure itself.

165
00:13:09,881 --> 00:13:13,740
As Franklin's lightning rods appeared on homes across America,

166
00:13:14,160 --> 00:13:17,940
an outraged clergy remained vehemently opposed to the invention.

167
00:13:17,980 --> 00:13:22,680
They believed that lightning expressed the wrath of God and should not be tampered with.

168
00:13:24,220 --> 00:13:30,600
As knowledge of electricity grew, inventors saw that this awesome force could be stored for later use.

169
00:13:31,160 --> 00:13:38,720
They developed a groundbreaking energy storage device that would ultimately fuel the communications industry, the battery.

170
00:13:39,760 --> 00:13:44,680
The battery has really an interesting sort of impact on the development of electricity.

171
00:13:44,681 --> 00:13:57,120
On one hand, it provides that basic building block that allows for experimentation in telegraphy, in telephony, and even in early wireless telegraph.

172
00:13:57,580 --> 00:14:00,680
But the search for a usable battery was not a new one.

173
00:14:01,620 --> 00:14:07,300
There is speculation that a crude form of electrical battery may have appeared in the first century A.D.

174
00:14:07,301 --> 00:14:17,560
In 1938, an Austrian scientist working in Baghdad uncovered a small pottery jar with a copper tube and iron rod running down its middle.

175
00:14:19,360 --> 00:14:26,220
Testing showed that the battery did emit small electrical charges, but not enough to provide any real source of power.

176
00:14:27,080 --> 00:14:31,280
Since it was discovered in what was clearly the house of a magician and medicine man, however,

177
00:14:31,281 --> 00:14:36,700
many others dismiss it as nothing more than a simple magic trick used to instill awe.

178
00:14:37,300 --> 00:14:40,000
perhaps even fear in all who saw it.

179
00:14:40,800 --> 00:14:43,680
The first authentic battery, as we know it,

180
00:14:43,720 --> 00:14:46,560
does not appear until the early 1800s

181
00:14:46,561 --> 00:14:49,140
when Italian inventor Alessandro Volta

182
00:14:49,141 --> 00:14:52,440
created the first viable energy storage device.

183
00:14:53,020 --> 00:14:55,820
What it consists of is a pile of plates.

184
00:14:56,220 --> 00:14:58,280
In fact, it's sometimes called a Volta's pile

185
00:14:58,281 --> 00:15:02,500
because it's literally a pile of metal plates

186
00:15:02,501 --> 00:15:06,080
alternating copper and nickel or copper and silver

187
00:15:06,081 --> 00:15:11,520
with an acid in between copper and zinc, another pile.

188
00:15:12,800 --> 00:15:16,660
And the bigger the pile you make, the more current you can get out of your battery.

189
00:15:17,460 --> 00:15:20,660
Volta's invention of the battery was truly groundbreaking.

190
00:15:21,240 --> 00:15:24,680
For the first time in history, the powerful force of electricity

191
00:15:24,681 --> 00:15:29,100
could actually be stored and used as a source of energy.

192
00:15:30,040 --> 00:15:34,780
In the middle of the 19th century, minor improvements were made to the battery.

193
00:15:35,520 --> 00:15:41,800
Now, known as wet cells, they basically consisted of a jar of acid with metal electrodes stuck inside.

194
00:15:42,060 --> 00:15:46,260
The acid caused a chemical reaction that produced electrons,

195
00:15:46,460 --> 00:15:50,560
which flowed through the metal electrodes to provide power to the attached device.

196
00:15:52,100 --> 00:15:54,860
Continual improvements were made to the battery.

197
00:15:55,620 --> 00:16:01,380
Inventors soon found it to be an indispensable device in their latest life-changing creations.

198
00:16:01,381 --> 00:16:09,380
The simple battery became a key element that ultimately fueled major advances in instantaneous communication.

199
00:16:09,381 --> 00:16:16,380
Less than 150 years ago, communications were mostly limited to messages delivered in person.

200
00:16:16,381 --> 00:16:22,380
As the scientific knowledge grew that electricity could be transferred from one place to another,

201
00:16:22,381 --> 00:16:29,380
inventors began to experiment with the possibility that the same principle might apply to communication.

202
00:16:29,381 --> 00:16:35,820
The result was a great invention that took the first steps toward bringing the world closer together.

203
00:16:36,540 --> 00:16:42,340
Instantaneous communication was now possible for the first time in history with the telegraph.

204
00:16:43,280 --> 00:16:50,360
The telegraph is based on the battery and the electromagnet, both of which are inventions of the very early 19th century.

205
00:16:50,361 --> 00:16:54,880
All you have to do to make a telegraph is simply string a wire over a distance,

206
00:16:54,881 --> 00:16:59,920
have an electromagnet attached to one end and have an on-off switch at the other end,

207
00:16:59,921 --> 00:17:01,920
and that allows you to send a message.

208
00:17:01,921 --> 00:17:06,720
And then when you apply a code, dots and dashes for example,

209
00:17:06,721 --> 00:17:11,280
so that you can represent numbers, letters of the alphabet, that sort of thing,

210
00:17:11,281 --> 00:17:16,680
then in fact you can send any message you want over any distance you want that you have the wires strung.

211
00:17:16,681 --> 00:17:27,020
Using his coding system of dots and dashes, American inventor Samuel Morse was the first to successfully send a telegraph message in 1844,

212
00:17:27,300 --> 00:17:31,620
with a demonstration line set up between Washington, D.C. and Baltimore.

213
00:17:32,300 --> 00:17:36,080
The message that Morse sent was a simple but powerful one.

214
00:17:36,580 --> 00:17:38,200
What hath God wrought?

215
00:17:39,200 --> 00:17:43,820
The growth of the electromagnetic telegraph was truly dramatic.

216
00:17:43,821 --> 00:17:51,200
In only 20 years after Morse had laid his first line between Washington and Baltimore in the mid-1840s,

217
00:17:51,520 --> 00:17:57,600
we had people putting in undersea Atlantic cable to transmit messages between continents.

218
00:17:58,000 --> 00:18:03,320
The battery-powered telegraph gave rise to a whole host of electrical inventions.

219
00:18:04,660 --> 00:18:10,060
If the telegraph sent the message that a communications renaissance was on the horizon,

220
00:18:10,061 --> 00:18:17,420
then it was one of history's greatest and most influential inventions that actually put it into words, the telephone.

221
00:18:18,380 --> 00:18:23,560
Telefender Graham Bell called it, in fact, a speaking telegraph initially because that's what it was.

222
00:18:23,600 --> 00:18:29,720
It was a telegraph line in which you had put a microphone and a speaker, in a sense, at each end

223
00:18:29,721 --> 00:18:34,220
so that, in fact, it would send the spoken voice rather than doff and dashes.

224
00:18:34,221 --> 00:18:40,880
Alexander Graham Bell had discovered that electrical currents could exactly duplicate sound waves,

225
00:18:40,881 --> 00:18:46,220
transmitting multiple sounds by vibrating in the air in a series of frequencies.

226
00:18:46,221 --> 00:18:53,220
Bell was a teacher for the deaf, and he had spent a great deal of time researching sound waves and the principles of acoustics.

